# ============================================================================
# TEMPORAL STABILITY - Reacting Only to a Stable Input
# ============================================================================
# Description: A rule whose output depends not just on the current input but on
# the input AND the output of the previous step. It reacts when the input has been
# high for two consecutive steps, and then holds - a single, isolated change does
# not flip it. This shows how history (both past input and past output) shapes the
# present.

# === STREAMS ===
i1 : sbf := in file("io/sequence.in")   # input signal, one bit per line
o1 : sbf := out file("io/stable.out")   # stabilized output

# === THE RULE ===
# The output o1[t] is 1 in exactly these three cases (an OR of them):
#   1) input is 1 now AND was 1 last step, and the output was still 0
#         (i1[t] & i1[t-1] & o1[t-1]')      -> rise once the input is stable-high
#   2) input is 0 now AND was 0 last step, and the output was already 1
#         (i1[t]' & i1[t-1]' & o1[t-1])     -> hold high through a stable-low stretch
#   3) the input just changed, and the output was already 1
#         ((i1[t] ^ i1[t-1]) & o1[t-1])     -> hold high across a single transition
r o1[t] = (i1[t] & i1[t-1] & o1[t-1]') | (i1[t]' & i1[t-1]' & o1[t-1]) | ((i1[t] ^ i1[t-1]) & o1[t-1])

# === HOW IT BEHAVES ===
# Look-back [t-1] means step 0 is an initialization step (output 0).
#
# Example: io/sequence.in = 1,1,0,0,1
#   step  : 0 1 2 3 4
#   input : 1 1 0 0 1
#   output: 0 1 1 1 1
# The output latches high at step 1 (input high for two steps in a row) and then
# holds through the following low and the change back to 1.
